93 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329482 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329482 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186328356 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329482 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ab6e4de6-e749-5260-9e23-6206beb6fb02" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329508 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186327355 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329508 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186328727 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329508 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186328943 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329508 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186329018 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329508 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186329023 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329508 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329508 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329508 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186327355 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329508 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "b8241441-5d37-5b80-8d02-80f7b14d12b8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329558 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186327799 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329558 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186327896 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329558 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186328152 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329558 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186328545 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329558 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186329024 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329558 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329558 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329558 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186327799 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329558 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d1f91c73-801b-5d0e-8fcf-e4a0d73b513d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329570 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186326964 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329570 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186327012 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329570 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186327713 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329570 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186328838 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329570 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186329011 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329570 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329570 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329570 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186329011 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329570 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "dc0b4dd2-5359-5754-b6be-a086a2742846" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329640 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186329235 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329640 #edb/ref 1069 "q-42264" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329640 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329640 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $A,B,$ and $C$ are $2\\times 2$ matrices, which of the following statements are always true?\n\n- $AB=BA$\n- $(AB)C=A(BC)$\n- $A(B-C)=(B-C)A$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329640 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "005a0156-3d99-59d1-ab99-adf9b9b4c7b4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329640 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's consider the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is false. Matrix multiplication is not commutative, so $AB\\ne BA$ in general.\n\n- Statement II is true. Matrix multiplication is associative, so $(AB)C=A(BC)$ in general.\n\n- Statement III is false. Matrix multiplication is not commutative, so $A(B-C)\\ne (B-C)A$ in general.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329689 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186329558 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329689 #edb/ref 1069 "q-42876" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329689 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329689 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $A=3I$, where $I$ is the $3\\times 3$ identity matrix, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $4\\text{det}({A}^{T})=108$\n- $\\text{det}(A)-6\\,\\text{det}(I)=0$\n- $\\text{det}({A}^{T})+\\text{det}(A)=54$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329689 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "35e600df-b2a1-5526-a22d-d7aabd8d69d2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329689 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3I & =3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe determinant of a diagonal matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}| & ={3}^{3}=27.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the properties of determinants, we know that \n\n$$\n\\text{det}({A}^{T})=\\text{det}(A)=27\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\text{det}(I)=1.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n4\\text{det}({A}^{T})=4\\cdot 27=108\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\text{det}(A)-6\\,\\text{det}(I)=27-6\\cdot 1=21\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\text{det}({A}^{T})+\\text{det}(A)=27+27=54\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329692 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186329312 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329692 #edb/ref 1069 "q-42886" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329692 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329692 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=sec⁡x?$\n\n- The point $(0,2)$ is in the graph\n- $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ is a vertical asymptote\n- $y=-2$ belongs to the range of the function." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329692 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3923a242-a394-5f37-9f6d-741dafd435c0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329692 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's plot the graph of $y=sec⁡x.$\n\n![](images/da/da729794a0331383debd75be0a4ce4a9f2d4da3286f50bd4f284868996077412.png)\n\nLet's go through each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. The $y$-intercept of $sec⁡x$ is $1.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function $y=sec⁡x$ has asymptotes at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ,$ thus $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ is a vertical asymptote.\n\n- Statement III is true. The range of the function is $(-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ),$ so $y=-2$ belongs to the range." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329816 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186329570 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329816 #edb/ref 1069 "q-47455" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329816 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329816 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=sec⁡x?$\n\n- The period of the function is $\\pi .$\n- The function is increasing in the interval $(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0].$\n- The function has no zeros." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329816 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4ce070dc-f77b-529a-a508-4630f3613570" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329816 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's plot the graph of $y=sec⁡x.$\n\n![](images/77/779d7a31646a229492a8165c8212a5c83ad7d37d843c1c1b3b36766a74bc5124.png)\n\nLet's go through each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. The period of the function is $2\\pi .$\n\n- Statement II is false. The function $y=sec⁡x$ is decreasing on $(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0].$\n\n- Statement III is true. The function has no zeros." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329821 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186329244 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329821 #edb/ref 1069 "q-47652" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329821 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329821 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $A=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -1 \\\\ -5 & k\\end{aligned}]$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],$ where $k$ is a constant such that $k\\ne 1.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0$\n- $Ax=0$ has no solution\n- The lines $5x-y=-1$ and $-5x+ky=0$ intersect exactly at one point" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329821 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c5470569-5538-5c85-aec6-dfcb3a6e366a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329821 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det[\\begin{aligned}5 & -1 \\\\ -5 & k\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =5\\cdot k-(-1)\\cdot (-5) \\\\  & =5k-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $5k-5\\ne 0$ if $k\\ne 1.$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the third part of the invertible matrix theorem, there must be a unique zero solution to $Ax=0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. According to the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem, the lines must intersect at exactly one point.\n\nFinally, only statements I and III are true." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329825 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186329196 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329825 #edb/ref 1069 "q-47679" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329825 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329825 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that that a function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=1$, has a jump discontinuity at $x=2$, and  is differentiable at $x=3$, which of the following statements must be true for **any** $f(x)$ that satisfy these criteria?\n\n- It is differentiable at $x=1$\n- It  is **not** differentiable at $x=2$\n- It is continuous at $x=3$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329825 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f90e0f73-a650-56a2-851e-2b406e202da6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329825 #edb/ref 1074 "- Statement I is false. Continuity does not imply differentiability. For example, the function $y=|x-1|$ is continuous but it's not differentiable at $x=1.$\n\n- Statement II is true.  A function that is discontinuous at a point can't be differentiable at this point (because differentiability implies continuity).\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, differentiability implies continuity." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329826 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186329187 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329826 #edb/ref 1069 "q-47681" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329826 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329826 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function given below?\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}+1, & x\\ne 1 \\\\ 3, & x=1\\end{aligned}\\,\n$$\n\nI. It is continuous and differentiable at $x=1$\n\nII. It is **not** differentiable at $x=1$\n\nIII. It is differentiable on the interval $(-1,2)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329826 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8298d153-fa1d-58c6-bfcd-efab6041f9f0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329826 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's analyze each statement individually. \n\n- Statement I is false.  At $x=1$, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}({x}^{3}+1)={1}^{3}+1=2 \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}({x}^{3}+1)={1}^{3}+1=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)\\ne f(1)=3,\n$$\n\nand so $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=1$ ($f(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=1$).\n\n- Statement II is true. Since  $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=1,$ it is also not differentiable there (because differentiability implies continuity).\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $f(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=1,$ it is not differentiable on any interval containing this point. Consequently, it is not differentiable on $(-1,2).$\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329885 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186329263 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329885 #edb/ref 1069 "q-48430" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329885 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329885 #edb/ref 1071 "A statement of the intermediate value theorem is given below but some of its parts are missing.\n\n*Let $f(x)$ be a function that is $\\text{ ______ }$ on a closed interval $[a,b].$ If $k$ is a number that lies between $\\text{ _________ }$, then there exists a number $c\\in [a,b]$ such that $f(c)=k.$*\n\nGoing from left to right, in which order should the parts I-III, listed below, be placed into the blank spaces to complete the statement of the theorem?\n\n- continuous\n- $f(a)$ and $f(b)$ inclusively\n- defined" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329885 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "74f6e633-2570-5d1a-abe0-fed2503f6040" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329885 #edb/ref 1074 "The intermediate value theorem states the following.\n\n*Let $f(x)$ be a function that is **continuous** on a closed interval $[a,b].$ If $k$ is a number that lies between $f(a)$ **and** $f(b)$ **inclusively**, then there exists a number $c\\in [a,b]$ such that $f(c)=k.$*\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329996 #edb/ref 1080 "I and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329996 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329996 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "04669e9c-9d0f-58e8-8793-3657d9ee41f0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186330073 #edb/ref 1080 "v=3,\\,w=-4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186330073 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186330073 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0e908bba-8bc6-5c10-9c84-aecd35277512" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186330194 #edb/ref 1080 "II and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186330194 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186330194 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1bd4d6b6-8ac8-54bc-9249-04799